Incorporated’s Description of Its Experience Cloud System \nScope and Boundaries of the System \nThis is a System and Organization Controls (SOC) 2 Type 2 report and includes a description of Adobe Incorporated’s \n(Adobe, service organization, or Company) Experience Cloud system (the system) and the controls in place to provide \nreasonable assurance that Adobe’s service commitments and system requirements were achieved based on the trust \nservices criteria relevant to security, availability, and confidentiality (applicable trust services criteria) set forth in \nTSP Section 100, 2017 Trust Services Criteria for Security, Availability, Processing Integrity, Confidentiality, and Privacy, \nin AICPA Trust Services Criteria, throughout the period November 1, 2022 to October 31, 2023 (the period), which may \nbe relevant to the users of the system. It does not encompass all aspects of the services provided or procedures \nfollowed for other activities performed by Adobe. \nBased on the nature of Adobe’s Experience Cloud system, mobile devices do not serve as information assets in relation \nto the system. As such, mobile device and security controls are not included in Section IV of this report. \nAdobe uses subservice organizations to perform certain services. A list of these subservice organizations and the \nservices performed is provided in the following table. The description does not disclose the actual controls at the \nsubservice organizations. \nSubservice Organization \nServices Performed \nAmazon Web Services, Inc. (AWS) \nProvides infrastructure as a service through its AWS platform. \nMicrosoft Corporation (Microsoft) \nProvides infrastructure as a service through its Azure platform. \nGoogle LLC \n(Adobe Workfront Only) Provides infrastructure as a service through its \nGoogle Cloud Platform (GCP). \nCyxtera Technologies, Inc. (Cyxtera) \nDigital Realty Trust, Inc. (formerly Interxion) \nEquinix, Inc. (Equinix) \nNTT ltd. (formerly Gyron) \nColocation providers that provide physical security and environmental \ncontrols over their colocation data centers. \nCompany Background \nFounded in 1982, Adobe is one of the largest and most diversified software companies in the world. Adobe offers a \nline of products and services to consumers for creating, managing, delivering, measuring, optimizing, and engaging \nwith compelling content and experiences across multiple operating systems, devices, and media. Adobe continues to \nmarket and license a broad portfolio of products and solutions in digital media, digital marketing, and content and \ndocument management. Adobe is a SEC-registered, publicly traded company and is subject to Sarbanes-Oxley (SOX) \n404 compliance. \nServices Provided \nThe Experience Cloud is a suite of digital marketing solutions. The Experience Cloud is a single platform that is \nsupported by many technologies, solutions, products, and services. \n=== PAGE 12 ===\n \nSection III \n\nThis document is CONFIDENTIAL AND PROPRIETARY to Adobe Incorporated and may not be reproduced, transmitted, published, or disclosed to others without Adobe Incorporated’s prior written consent. \nIt may ONLY be used for the purpose for which it is provided. \n12 \nExperience Cloud customers can manage all their different Experience Cloud solutions from a single landing page, on \nwhich they have access to relevant metrics, dashboards, and reports. Customers can grant access to other users to \nshare their Experience Cloud data. The Adobe Experience Cloud platform also acts as a central hub for sharing assets \nbetween Adobe Experience Cloud customers and “Adobe Creatives,” who are users that generate digital content via \nthe Adobe Experience Cloud and Adobe Experience Cloud solution and service offerings. \nThe core solutions of the Adobe Experience Cloud are briefly described in the table below: \nExperience Cloud \nSolution \nDescription \nAdobe Campaign \nAdobe Campaign is a managed service that allows customers to configure, manage, and \ncustomize communication, reporting and data channels. Campaign offers a suite of \nfunctionality including rule-based marketing offers, transactional message delivery, \npersonalized promotional communications, cross-channel campaign management, lead \nmanagement tools, social marketing, and resource management. The Campaign platform \nallows customers to customize, control, and integrate their Campaign platform into \nexisting processes via an Integrated Development Environment (IDE). \nAdobe Experience \nManager \nAdobe Experience Manager (AEM) allows customers to create, publish, and manage \ncustomized, marketable site content, mobile applications, and social media presence. \nCustomers can publish dynamic e-catalogs and import organic, user-generated social \nmedia content. Video files of any format can be transcoded, customized, and published. \nCustomers can create personalized, targeted email campaigns and publish social media \ncontent or AEM assets to their website and social media accounts in an ad hoc or \nscheduled manner. \nAdobe Experience \nManager Cloud Service \nAdobe Experience Manager Cloud Service (AEMCS) provides a scalable, secure, and agile \ntechnology foundation, enabling marketers and IT to focus on delivering impactful \nexperiences at scale. The modern cloud-native architecture of Experience Manager Cloud \nService is built upon a container-based infrastructure, application programming interface \n(API)-driven development, and guided DevOps process, allowing IT to focus on strategic \nbusiness outcomes instead of getting slowed down by operational concerns. \n \nAdobe Experience Manager Cloud Service uses Cloud Manager to give developers the \nability to create impactful customer experiences. Continuous integration and continuous \ndelivery/continuous deployment (CI/CD) pipelines optimized for Adobe Experience \nManager allow you to easily merge development workflows by simply checking in your \ncode, which can then move all the way to being production ready. Cloud Manager uses an \nopen API approach and enables you to integrate with your systems without disrupting \nexisting processes and tools. \nAdobe Primetime \nAdobe Primetime allows premium content providers, cable operators, and programmers \nto drive value for paid TV services and strengthen brand affinity for content owners. Secure \nworkflows for live, linear, and video-on-demand programming deliver an engaging, \npersonalized viewing experience on IP-connected devices. Adobe Primetime offers a suite \nof functionality to customers including, but not limited to, digital rights management, a \nvideo player software development kit, subscriber management (e.g., subscriber \nauthentication prior to viewing), advertisement delivery, and analysis tools. \n=== PAGE 13 ===\n \nSection III \n\nThis document is CONFIDENTIAL AND PROPRIETARY to Adobe Incorporated and may not be reproduced, transmitted, published, or disclosed to others without Adobe Incorporated’s prior written consent. \nIt may ONLY be used for the purpose for which it is provided. \n13 \nExperience Cloud \nSolution \nDescription \nAdobe Target \nAdobe Target conducts real-time, customizable website marketing and content campaigns \nvia A/B testing, rule-based content delivery, and multivariate testing. Campaigns can be \ntargeted against specific segments of website traffic and visitor audiences. Adobe Target \nevaluates web-surfer click paths to determine the most relevant content to display. \nCustomers can rely on the systematic evaluation of website structure and web-surfer \nactivity for Adobe Target to determine the most effective marketing content to display. \nAdobe Analytics \nAdobe Analytics ingests targeted website traffic and executes robust reporting and \nanalytics based on customizable configurations. An array of functionality is available, \nincluding custom dashboards, ad hoc analytics, Microsoft Excel integration, bulk data \nimport, data enrichment, tag management, and data integrations between Adobe and \nselect partners. Data can be exported for external purposes or integrated with other Adobe \nproducts to enrich existing datasets.  \nAdobe Customer \nJourney Analytics \nCustomer Journey Analytics transforms the Analytics product line into a fully automated \ncloud native service for customers that want a SaaS-like experience. Customer Journey \nAnalytics is an Analytics capability that lets you use the power of Analysis Workspace with \ndata from Adobe Experience Platform to expand the scope of Analytics by offering easy-to-\nuse cross-channel capabilities.  \nAdobe Audience \nManager \nAdobe Audience Manager allows customers to design and build web-traffic segments, \nknown as “audiences,” based on customer visitor website interactions. Rulesets are \nconfigured to build complex audiences based on customer-defined signals, (e.g., \npage=“sports” or event=“form submission”) traits (i.e., a combination of signals) and \nsegments (i.e., an aggregation of both traits and signals). These combined data elements \nare used to create audiences, which are used to drive focused and targeted advertising \ncampaigns. \nAdobe Advertising Cloud \nAdobe Advertising Cloud provides advertisers with the capability to assess, forecast, and \noptimize online advertising spend across channels. Advertisers can manage ad campaigns \nfor search engines, websites, and social media platforms. Data from pixel tracking, \npublisher-provided reports, and advertiser-provided revenue feeds are processed by \nalgorithms that manipulate advertising spend and configuration decisions for ad \ncampaigns, which are delivered to real-time and future, bidding-based publishers.  \nAdobe Connect \nAdobe Connect is an enterprise-level, web-based conferencing and collaboration solution \nfor online meetings and interactions. Adobe Connect supports audio and video streaming \nfor live camera and voice conversation, enables the presentation of video and audio files, \nfacilitates screen sharing, and allows for collaborative white-boarding. Online sessions can \nbe recorded, and recordings can be reviewed, edited, and posted for playback by \nauthorized users. \nAdobe Experience \nPlatform  \nAdobe Experience Platform is a purpose-built platform for customer experience \nmanagement that helps users collect, connect, and activate customer data from across \nsources, channels, and customer interactions in real time to create robust, unified customer \nprofiles. Adobe Experience Platform standardizes data for intelligence and profile creation \nand provides an open and extensible cloud infrastructure, real-time updates, AI-driven \ninsights, and scalability, with a wide variety of supporting products and services. \n=== PAGE 14 ===\n \nSection III \n\nThis document is CONFIDENTIAL AND PROPRIETARY to Adobe Incorporated and may not be reproduced, transmitted, published, or disclosed to others without Adobe Incorporated’s prior written consent. \nIt may ONLY be used for the purpose for which it is provided. \n14 \nExperience Cloud \nSolution \nDescription \nReal-time CDP \nReal-Time CDP (Customer Data Platform) is an application built on Adobe Experience \nPlatform and delivers real-time personalization at scale to enable brands to bring together \ninternal and external, known and unknown customer data to activate real-time customer \nand account profiles that allow for B2C and B2B marketers to deliver timely, relevant \nexperiences across channels. \nAdobe Journey \nOptimizer \nAdobe Journey Optimizer, natively built on Adobe Experience Platform, allows companies \nto create and deliver contextual and personalized experiences for omnichannel campaigns \nacross the customer journey. With Adobe Journey Optimizer, companies can use real-time \ncustomer behavior and intelligence decisioning insights to determine the next best \ninteraction for an optimized-in-the moment engagement with their customers. \nAdobe Commerce \nBusiness Intelligence \nBusiness Intelligence empowers merchants to easily harness the power of your Adobe \nCommerce database along with your other systems to accelerate smart data-driven \ndecisions. Business Intelligence provides merchants the capabilities to analyze what is \nimportant with just one eCommerce solution. Automated data syncs update dashboards \nthroughout the day, so merchants can have confidence in their eCommerce analytics \naround the clock. \nAdobe Commerce Order \nManagement \nOrder Management helps businesses with multiple brands, sites, stores, warehouses, etc., \ncreate seamless cross-channel experiences. Order Management helps merchants bridge \nthe gap between their digital and physical worlds, from sites and stores to warehouses. \nAdobe Marketo Engage \nEngage empowers customers to attract buyers and deliver win-ready leads to sales. \nEngage helps customers prioritize leads and provides the ability to convert leads into sales \nby delivering automated, personalized campaigns that deliver a cohesive and compelling \nstory across all channels. \n \nSales Connect empowers sales representatives with a single workflow for more automated \nand personalized inbound and outbound engagements. Sales Connect provides a \nprioritized listing of the highest-scoring leads supported with real-time insights from both \nsales and marketing teams. \nAdobe Marketo Measure \n(Bizible) \nMarketo Measure (Bizible) measures the impact of marketing strategies and tactics to \nimprove overall ROI and validate marketing investments. Marketo Measure provides \ngreater visibility into full-funnel marketing, allowing organizations to seamlessly link their \nrevenue back to marketing spend. \n=== PAGE 15 ===\n \nSection III \n\nThis document is CONFIDENTIAL AND PROPRIETARY to Adobe Incorporated and may not be reproduced, transmitted, published, or disclosed to others without Adobe Incorporated’s prior written consent. \nIt may ONLY be used for the purpose for which it is provided. \n15 \nExperience Cloud \nSolution \nDescription \nAdobe Workfront \nAdobe Workfront is a work management platform that companies use to manage their \nentire lifecycle of work, in one place. Built for people, Adobe Workfront is intuitive, flexible, \ncustomizable, and personalized to how teams operate. The Workfront application is a SaaS \nsolution that provides enterprise teams a single, centralized place to manage and \ncollaborate on their work. With Adobe Workfront, teams, managers, and executives readily \nget real-time visibility into work planning, prioritization, resourcing, and workflow to help \neveryone work more efficiently toward achieving the organization’s goals. In 2016, Adobe \nWorkfront added an extension that allows for integration with the Adobe Creative Cloud. \nAdobe Workfront helps achieve these goals by offering a solution that facilitates the \nfollowing: \n• \nAll work in one place \n• \nObjective-centered planning \n• \nAutomating busywork \n• \nVisibility via collaboration \n• \nOptimization via visibility \n \nProof is a module within Adobe Workfront that allows teams to track rich media, organize \ncollaboration, and automate approvals. \n \nWorkfront Fusion consists of a visual workflow designer that allows power users to \nconnect Adobe Workfront to any other application with a public Representational State \nTransfer (REST) API, without writing any code. It supports various data processing and \ntransformation functions and has built-in connectors to hundreds of popular services. \nPrincipal Service Commitments and System Requirements \nIn order to meet with security, availability, and confidentiality commitments of the services offered, Adobe has \nimplemented several technology, process, and physical controls across its environments. Adobe documents and \ncommunicates its service commitments to its customers in form of its service-level agreements (SLAs) and contracts \nwith customers. Service commitments include, but are not limited to: \nSecurity \n• \nDesigning and implementing access control measures so that the fewest number of operators have access \nto data. \n• \nDefining and deploying role-based access to restrict privileged access to information resources based on the \nconcept of least privilege. \n• \nUsing a monitoring solution to identify any spikes in activity above a predefined critical security and \navailability threshold. \n• \nEstablishing trigger alerts for identified anomalies, and then using established procedures to address them \nand any potential security threats they may represent. \n=== PAGE 16 ===\n \nSection III \n\nThis document is CONFIDENTIAL AND PROPRIETARY to Adobe Incorporated and may not be reproduced, transmitted, published, or disclosed to others without Adobe Incorporated’s prior written consent. \nIt may ONLY be used for the purpose for which it is provided. \n16 \n• \nDesigning and implementing processes to ensure that changes to the network or production environment \nare documented, tracked, tested, authorized, and approved prior to migration to production. \n• \nUsing a monitoring solution to evaluate and check logs for potential misconfigurations. \n• \nUsing encryption technologies to protect customer data and/or assets, both at rest and in transit. \n• \nExecuting periodic vulnerability scans and penetration tests to identify vulnerabilities. Tracking and \nremediating them on a risk basis using defined SLAs. \nAvailability \n• \nDesigning and implementing controls to ensure system availability. \n• \nEstablishing business continuity and disaster recovery mechanisms to minimize data loss and ensure return \nto operations in the case of a disaster, using industry-standard mechanisms. \n• \nTesting disaster recovery plans annually. \n• \nEstablishing mechanisms to notify its customers of potential operational issues that could impact the service \navailability. \nConfidentiality \n• \nEstablishing commitments for customer data confidentiality and for data deletion and retention, as \nappropriate, within the customer contracts. \n• \nDesigning and implementing controls to ensure that data is kept confidential, retained, and deleted per \ncustomer contracts. \nCustomers have the ability to contact Adobe for product support, sales, and security-related queries through various \nchannels, including phone and email. Important information regarding security vulnerabilities that could affect specific \nversions of Adobe products and services is published on the Adobe Security Bulletin and Advisory website. An Adobe \nservice status dashboard is maintained and made available at all times to its customers. Current status information \ncan be checked by the customer on the service status dashboard. \nAdobe also establishes operational requirements that support the achievement of these service commitments, \nrelevant laws and regulations, and other systems requirements. These requirements are communicated in Adobe’s \npolicies and standards documentation. Policies define an organizationwide approach to how systems and data are \nprotected and made available. These include policies around how the service is designed and developed, how the \nsystem is operated, how the internal business systems and networks are managed, and how employees are hired \nand trained. \nSystem Incidents \nA system incident is an incident that leads to the loss of, or disruption to, operations, services, or functions and results \nin Adobe’s failure to achieve its service commitments or system requirements. Such an occurrence may arise from a \nsecurity event, security incident, failure to comply with applicable laws and regulations, error, or other means. In \ndetermining whether a system incident occurred resulting in Adobe’s failure to achieve one or more of its service \ncommitments or system requirements, considerations may include, but are not limited to, the following: \n=== PAGE 17 ===\n \nSection III \